      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          RMR-141 is a genuine EPA-registered fungicide, and it kills mold well when you follow one instruction most people skip: keep the surface wet for a full 10 minutes. It will not remove stains and it cannot fix damp, so the honest verdict is that it is the right first step, not the whole job.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What RMR-141 Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            RMR-141 is a ready-to-use disinfectant, fungicide and cleaner sold by RMR Brands, the same company behind the RMR-86 stain remover that shows up in so many before-and-after videos. The detail that separates it from most of the mold sprays on a hardware store shelf is printed on its safety data sheet: EPA Registration No. 61178-2-93443. That number means the product's kill claims were submitted to the Environmental Protection Agency under federal pesticide law, and the directions on the label are the conditions under which those claims hold. A cleaner without a registration number can say it removes mold or mildew; it cannot legally claim to kill it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The active ingredients are two quaternary ammonium compounds at 0.07 percent each, for 0.14 percent combined. Quaternary ammonium, usually shortened to quats, is the same family of chemistry used in many hospital and food-service disinfectants. Quats work by disrupting the cell membranes of microorganisms, and they do it without the oxidizing action of chlorine bleach. That is why RMR-141 does not whiten fabric, does not have a pool-chemical smell, and is far less likely to corrode metal fittings or discolor wood than a bleach spray. The trade-off, which comes up again and again in this review, is that quats need time to work, and they do nothing about the color of a stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            The retail version most people buy is labeled RTU, which stands for ready to use. There is nothing to mix: you spray it straight from the bottle. Home Depot's listing for the gallon describes it as killing 99.9 percent of household bacteria and viruses and containing no abrasives, bleach or harsh acids. RMR also sells a Pro concentrate aimed at remediation contractors, covered in the formats section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            It helps to be clear about what kind of review this is. We have not run a lab test on RMR-141, and nothing on this page pretends otherwise. What follows is drawn from the label, the safety data sheet, the maker's own claims, EPA guidance on mold cleanup and what owners commonly report. For a product whose entire value rests on following its directions exactly, the label is the most useful source there is, and most of the complaints about RMR-141 we have read trace back to someone who skipped a step it spells out.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">RMR-141 at a Glance (Label and SDS)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">61178-2-93443</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EPA Reg. No.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.14%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Total quats (2 x 0.07%)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wet contact time for mold</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">140+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Microorganisms, per maker</p>
