        <div id="rmr-86-vs-141" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. RMR-141 vs RMR-86: Kill First, Then Clean
          </h2>

          <p>
            The question of RMR 86 vs RMR 141 comes up constantly, because the two bottles sit side by side, carry the same brand, and are often sold as a bundle. They are not alternatives. They do two different jobs, and RMR is unusually candid about it: its own FAQ states that RMR-86 does not kill mold; it removes the stains caused by mold and mildew. RMR-141 is the product RMR sells to kill mold, and it does not remove stains. Each one covers the other's gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            The chemistry explains the split. RMR-86 is a bleach-based product: its safety data sheet lists sodium hypochlorite at 1 to 5 percent, which is why it strips dark staining off a wall in seconds and also why it has a strong chlorine smell. Its directions call for spraying from 6 to 8 inches until the area is covered, letting it work for at least 15 seconds, and rinsing once the stains have gone. RMR lists decks, wood, vinyl, siding, drywall, concrete and brick as suitable and warns against metals and fabrics. It also recommends a full-face respirator with appropriate cartridges, a chemical suit and nitrile gloves, which tells you how different its hazard profile is from RMR-141's.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
